 and also close  off on some thoughts on her approach to  feeding her kids let's take a look for  breakfast i made this vanilla protein  oatmeal with banana blueberries toasted  coconut flakes peanut butter and a  little bit of honey okay this is amazing  looking so she said protein oats so i  assume we've got protein powder in there  and we've obviously got lots of fiber in  the oats and berries plus healthy fats  in the nut butters and coconut this is  textbook hunger crushing combo here  and almost too pretty to eat almost in  my protein shake there's blueberries  greek yogurt banana almond milk and  protein powder okay so this is pretty  similar to the oatmeal bowl but without  the spoon so we're getting protein from  the protein powder and yogurt plus carbs  and fiber from the fruit and depending  on the type of yogurt we may also have  some fat now i do get asked a lot about  low versus no versus full fat dairy and  ultimately it's a personal choice but i  do always recommend full if you like it  i think it's more delicious that's  number one and more satiating thanks to  the fat and contrary to what you might  think the research actually suggests  that high fat dairy is associated with  lower risk of weight gain and chronic  disease now this is a quickie short  video so i don't know if these meals are  also being shared with her kids and also  in another video which i'm going to  comment on in a minute i did see that  she made her kids a smoothie without  protein powder but just as a teachable  moment for those of you watching i  generally do recommend avoiding protein  powder for kids it's rarely actually  necessary nutritionally since kids  protein needs are actually quite low  less than 20 grams for kids four to nine  years old for example which honestly  isn't that hard to get even for the days  they like live on carbs but supplements  like protein powders are generally quite  poorly regulated and not all are well  tested for impurities heavy metals  and 